1
00:00:00,420 --> 00:00:02,246
Book Four, Proposition Five.

2
00:00:02,246 --> 00:00:05,377
About a given triangle to circumscribe a circle.

3
00:00:05,377 --> 00:00:11,770
Let A B C be the given triangle; it is required to circumscribe a circle about the triangle A B C.

4
00:00:12,190 --> 00:00:23,133
Bisect the sides A B and A C at the points D and E by One Ten, and from D and E draw D F and E F at right angles to A B and A C.

5
00:00:23,133 --> 00:00:30,314
They will meet either within the triangle A B C, or on the line B C, or outside B C.

6
00:00:30,314 --> 00:00:32,280
Join F A, F B, and F C.

7
00:00:32,700 --> 00:00:41,643
Because A D equals D B, and D F is common and at right angles, by Side-Angle-Side congruence the base A F equals F B.

8
00:00:41,643 --> 00:00:51,656
By the exact same reasoning on side A C, C F also equals A F, so the three segments F A, F B, and F C are all equal to one another.

9
00:00:51,656 --> 00:01:09,695
Therefore the circle described with center F and radius F A passes through A, B, and C, and is circumscribed about the triangle A B C—with angle B A C acute when F falls inside, right when F lies on B C, and obtuse when F falls outside.

10
00:01:09,695 --> 00:01:11,300
Which was to be done.

11
00:01:11,720 --> 00:01:25,193
Proposition Four Five establishes the circumcenter of any triangle as the intersection of two perpendicular bisectors, unifying all three cases—acute, right, and obtuse—under Thales' semicircle theorem, Three Thirty-One.

12
00:01:25,193 --> 00:01:35,911
As Simson and Todhunter observed, the perpendicular bisectors of two non-collinear sides must always meet because lines perpendicular to intersecting lines cannot be parallel.

13
00:01:35,911 --> 00:01:51,160
